1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,500
Crossing dimensions can change the world on screen.

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:06,400
The data needed to draw the new world must also be prepared.

3
00:00:06,400 --> 00:00:10,040
Follow the PC team’s account of preparing the required data.

4
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:13,480
Games need data such as models and textures.

5
00:00:13,480 --> 00:00:14,800
If it is stored in compressed form,

6
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:16,500
it must be decompressed after reading.

7
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:19,460
The PC edition uses DirectStorage.

8
00:00:19,460 --> 00:00:23,720
At high graphics settings, the GPU also assists decompression.

9
00:00:23,720 --> 00:00:27,200
Decompression was typically handled by the CPU.

10
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:29,880
The graphics device can help with that work.

11
00:00:29,880 --> 00:00:31,300
Data needs to be prepared in the background

12
00:00:31,300 --> 00:00:34,600
so it is available after crossing dimensions.

13
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:37,960
Reading and decompression are distinct stages.

14
00:00:37,960 --> 00:00:40,940
Storage speeds differ between PCs.

15
00:00:40,940 --> 00:00:44,240
The developers examine available bandwidth

16
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:46,900
and adapt the streaming strategy.

17
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:48,900
Fast and slow storage devices

18
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:52,000
do not always receive the same data-supply plan.

19
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,320
The feature is not limited to fast storage.

20
00:00:55,320 --> 00:00:59,000
The official PC guidance also lists hard drive support.

21
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,300
It recommends an SSD for a better experience.

22
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:06,600
Reading, decompression, and supply adjusted to storage speed work together.
